  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The graph of $y=f(-(x+5))$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Translation by $5$ units to the left\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Stretch of scale factor $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Reflection across the $y$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?",
  "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=f(-(x+5)),$ we can apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4):$ Reflect $y=f(x)$ across the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(-x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2):$ Translate $y=f(-x)$ to the left by $5$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(-(x+5)).\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2).$",
